target/ppc: don't take the BQL in ppc_maybe_interrupt() for nothing

Every MSR store (mtmsr, rfi, wrteei...) calls ppc_maybe_interrupt(),
which took the BQL to raise or withdraw CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD. Mac OS X
toggles MSR[EE] constantly, and with 16 vCPUs 6.9% of vCPU thread
time went to waiting for the BQL there.

With no interrupt pending and none requested there is nothing to do.
Pending bits are only set under the BQL by ppc_set_irq(), which then
re-checks the MSR: with a full barrier on each side between the store
and the load, one of the two sees the other's update, so skipping the
lock when both are clear can't lose an interrupt.


diff --git a/target/ppc/excp_helper.c b/target/ppc/excp_helper.c
index 8de8fce779..cac0ba1df8 100644
--- a/target/ppc/excp_helper.c
+++ b/target/ppc/excp_helper.c
@@ -2299,7 +2299,23 @@ static int ppc_next_unmasked_interrupt(CPUPPCState *env)
 void ppc_maybe_interrupt(CPUPPCState *env)
 {
     CPUState *cs = env_cpu(env);
+
+    /*
+     * With nothing pending and no interrupt requested there is nothing to
+     * raise or withdraw, so an MSR store needn't take the BQL. Pending bits
+     * are only set under the BQL by ppc_set_irq(), which then re-checks the
+     * MSR: with a barrier on each side between the store and the load, one
+     * of the two sees the other's update.
+     */
+    if (!bql_locked()) {
+        smp_mb();
+        if (!qatomic_read(&env->pending_interrupts) &&
+            !cpu_test_interrupt(cs, CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD)) {
+            return;
+        }
+    }
     BQL_LOCK_GUARD();
+    smp_mb(); /* pairs with the one above, after ppc_set_irq()'s store */
 
     if (ppc_next_unmasked_interrupt(env)) {
         cpu_interrupt(cs, CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD);
